[{"_1":2,"_3":4},"root",{"_5":113},"routes/item",{"_5":6},"data",{"_7":8,"_9":10},"item",{"_11":12,"_13":14,"_15":16,"_17":18,"_19":-5,"_20":21,"_22":23,"_24":25,"_26":27,"_28":27,"_29":30,"_31":32,"_33":34,"_35":36,"_37":38,"_39":40,"_41":-5,"_42":43,"_44":45,"_46":47,"_48":49,"_50":51,"_52":53,"_54":36,"_55":56,"_57":58,"_59":36,"_60":61},"expiresAt",1795567279000,"id","floi46t5sltnh30ocwuxh7729","title","Rapid7 MDR Evaluation Scorecard：如何用18项检查比较MDR服务商","originalTitle","How to compare MDR providers when every proposal looks promising","summary","Rapid7 MDR Evaluation Scorecard 提供18项检查，涵盖暴露感知检测、情报、响应责任与成本、透明度、团队 expertise 和集成，帮助企业比较MDR服务商及续约方案。评估时应要求以匿名案例展示完整调查，用同一标准核对服务层级、集成范围、响应分工和总成本，并追踪改进措施；整体得分高不能替代组织必需但方案未覆盖的响应能力。","reason","source",{"_74":112},"links",{"_61":111},"publishedAt","2026-11-24T15:51:25.000Z","discoveredAt","2026-11-24T17:01:38.792Z","timelineAt","category","blue-team","tags",[107,108,109,81,110,79,45],"score",29,"selected",false,"channel","news","story",{"_85":86,"_13":87},"x","readingMode","full","author","Rapid7","body",{"_103":-5,"_61":104,"_105":-5,"_106":36},"outline",[88,89,90,91],"relatedStories",[84],"topics",[68,69,70,71,72],"indexable","markdownAvailable",true,"group",{"_62":63,"_64":65,"_66":67},"hasTranslation","bodyLanguage","original","factId","f2Preaj7Qjv4","reportCount",1,"additionalSourceCount",0,{"_73":82,"_74":83},{"_73":80,"_74":81},{"_73":78,"_74":79},{"_73":76,"_74":77},{"_73":30,"_74":75},"slug","name","蓝队与运营","tools","工具与平台","vuln-mgmt","漏洞管理","incident","应急响应","rapid7","Rapid7 / Metasploit",{"_85":86,"_13":87},"publicId","3b92f650-0656-4292-bf04-13c33267e3ff","Rapid7发布MDR提供商评估评分卡",{"_11":101,"_93":102,"_95":96},{"_11":99,"_93":100,"_95":96},{"_11":97,"_93":98,"_95":96},{"_11":92,"_93":94,"_95":96},"sec-4","text","Turn the answers into a decision","level",2,"sec-3","Look for improvement you can trace","sec-2","Compare the service you would actually receive","sec-1","Start with one investigation","zh","<p><span>Choosing a managed detection and response (MDR) provider means committing budget, connecting systems, and trusting another team to act when your organization is under pressure. Yet a proposal promising 24x7 coverage, expert analysts, and fast response can leave important questions unanswered. Which systems will actually be monitored? What will analysts investigate before contacting you? What happens when an incident needs more work?</span></p><p><span>Before choosing a provider or renewing your current agreement, ask for evidence of how the service operates. Rapid7’s </span><a href=\"https://www.rapid7.com/resources/mdr-scorecard/\"><span>MDR Evaluation Scorecard</span></a><span> gives you 18 checks to structure that conversation and record what still needs proving.</span></p><h2 id=\"sec-1\">Start with one investigation</h2><p><span>Ask a provider to walk you through a completed investigation, using an anonymized example where necessary. Follow the initial signal through the evidence considered, the analyst’s decision, and any response actions.</span></p><p><span>Suppose suspicious activity involves an account with access to a critical business system. Can the provider show how the account’s permissions, the systems within reach, and known weaknesses influenced its investigation? Can it explain what it ruled out and why it escalated or contained the activity?</span></p><p><span>This exercise helps you assess whether exposure context, intelligence, and customer telemetry work together. It also makes the division of work visible: what the provider investigated, what it decided, and what your team had to do next.</span></p><h2 id=\"sec-2\">Compare the service you would actually receive</h2><p><span>Use the same requirements for every provider, including your incumbent. Confirm the proposed service tier, supported integrations, response responsibilities, and total costs before comparing scores.</span></p><p><span>Be specific about incident response. Establish who handles containment, forensics, eradication, and mitigation, which actions require your approval, and whether additional work changes the bill. A demonstration should be supported by written commitments about the service you would receive.</span></p><p><a href=\"https://www.rapid7.com/services/managed-detection-and-response-mdr/\"><span>Rapid7 MDR</span></a><span> includes unlimited incident response, giving customers direct access to senior responders for digital forensics, eradication, and mitigation. When evaluating that commitment, ask us to explain the activities covered, how containment and remediation are coordinated, and which decisions remain with your team.</span></p><p><span>Apply the same scrutiny to intelligence. Ask for an example showing how an emerging threat led to detection updates, hunting activity, or customer guidance. Rapid7’s emerging-threat response process connects research, detection engineering, product, and communications. The useful evaluation question is how that process changes the protection and advice customers receive.</span></p><h2 id=\"sec-3\">Look for improvement you can trace</h2><p><span>A detailed investigation record helps you understand what happened. Your next question should be what changed afterward.</span></p><p><span>Look for recommendations that led to stronger controls, better detections, or closed coverage gaps. Ask how the provider reviews completed actions and recurring problems with your team. When comparing reporting, check whether you can trace those improvements alongside investigation volumes and response measures.</span></p><h2 id=\"sec-4\">Turn the answers into a decision</h2><p><span>The scorecard covers six areas: exposure-aware detection, intelligence, response ownership and cost, transparency, team expertise, and integration. Rate each capability against evidence, then record limitations, outstanding questions, and follow-up owners.</span></p><p><span>The total helps organize your comparison, but essential requirements deserve individual attention. A strong overall score cannot compensate for a response capability your organization needs and the proposed service excludes.</span></p><p><span>Download the </span><a href=\"https://www.rapid7.com/resources/mdr-scorecard/\"><span>MDR Evaluation Scorecard</span></a><span> before your next provider review, shortlist meeting, or renewal discussion. Use it to make the evidence comparable and identify exactly what needs resolving before you commit.</span></p>","zhKind","complete","蓝队","工具评测","检测工程","安全运营","https://www.rapid7.com/blog/post/dr-comparing-mdr-providers-scorecard","Rapid7 CVE 公告",{"_114":115,"_116":-5},"changelogVersion","2026-10-01T08:00","release"]
